before:
```typescript
type Parcel = { label: string; weight: number };
function parcelLabel(parcel: Parcel): string {
  return parcel.label + ": " + parcel.weight;
}
console.log(parcelLabel({ label: "Seeds", weight: 3 }));
```

This prints Seeds: 3. parcelLabel receives an object with the Parcel shape, reads its two fields, and returns a string. The object literal inside the call supplies those fields. The function does not change the object or construct a new parcel.

after:
This function reads the two properties and returns a label.
```typescript
type Parcel = { label: string; weight: number };
function parcelLabel(parcel: Parcel): string {
  return parcel.label + ": " + parcel.weight;
}
console.log(parcelLabel({ label: "Seeds", weight: 3 }));
```
The output is Seeds: 3. The original object stays unchanged.
